Recent measurements of the branching fraction and pT spectrum for the process $\Lambda_b 	o p 	au^-  ar{
u}_	au$ have been scrutinized, with the observed suppression of events—particularly those high in transverse momentum—suggesting the influence of new physics. This work examines how recent data on the rare decay $b 	o c 	au^-  ar{
u}_	au$ can constrain predictions for the $ \Lambda_b 	o p 	au^-  ar{
u}_	au $ decay within a specific class of scalar leptoquark models. The analysis indicates that the parameter space permitted by $b 	o c 	o 	ext{same-flavor} $ data is largely incompatible with the limits derived from the $ 	au $-neutrino channel. Consequently, this model space is further restricted by upcoming experimental results from LHCb and Belle II regarding $b 	o c 	o 	ext{same-flavor} $ transitions.
